<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!--
	  MultiSoundChanger is a pure Swift/AppKit menu-bar app. The only Hardened-Runtime
	  exception we need is `com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation`, because:

	    • CocoaPods' `use_frameworks!` embeds MediaKeyTap into the .app bundle as a dynamic
	      framework.
	    • We sign ad-hoc (`CODE_SIGN_IDENTITY = "-"`) for open-source source-built use.
	    • Hardened Runtime's default library validation rejects embedded dylibs unless they
	      share a Team ID with the main binary — ad-hoc signatures have no team, so the
	      archived .app refuses to launch embedded frameworks (test builds from Xcode work
	      because Launch Services is more permissive for direct-run-from-DerivedData).

	  Narrowly-scoped exception: only affects which dylibs can load into this process. Real
	  risk requires the attacker to already have write access to the .app bundle, at which
	  point they have worse options than dylib injection.

	  NOT adding:
	    • App Sandbox — CGEventTap (via MediaKeyTap) requires host access.
	    • `com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit` / `allow-unsigned-executable-memory` — no JIT.
	    • `com.apple.security.automation.apple-events` — NSWorkspace uses LaunchServices.
	    • `com.apple.security.cs.allow-dyld-environment-variables` — we don't use DYLD vars.

	  If shipping via Developer ID + notarization later, this exception can be REMOVED if
	  all embedded frameworks get re-signed with the same Developer ID (which CocoaPods'
	  standard xcconfig handles). But for source-built ad-hoc distribution, it stays.
	-->
	<key>com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation</key>
	<true/>
</dict>
</plist>
